{"model": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Answer the user question using only the selected evidence below.\nDo not assume facts that are absent from the selected evidence. If the evidence is\ninsufficient, return UNKNOWN rather than inventing a state or action.\n\nSelected evidence:\n<evidence_session_01>\n[user] Can you help me draft a quick text to a friend and maybe a simple plan for coordinating pickup/drop-off? I’m trying to get my weekend sorted and I just realized I still have Mike's pressure washer; I need to get it back to him. I don’t want it to sound awkward or like I forgot for ages—just friendly and to the point.\n[assistant] Sure, here’s a simple, friendly message you could send:\n\n“Hey Mike! I just realized I still have your pressure washer. I’m around this weekend if you want me to drop it off, or I can meet you somewhere that’s easier. Just let me know what works for you.”\n\nFor the plan, you can keep it casual: once he replies with a time, offer a nearby spot or just say you’ll swing by his place. If timing gets tricky, you can suggest leaving it on his porch or in his driveway so you don’t have to coordinate face‑to‑face.\n\nIf you want it even shorter or more casual, I can tweak it.\n[user] Could you give me 2–3 variations of that text (one super short, one a bit more “sorry about that” without being weird, and one that offers porch/driveway drop-off)? Also, what’s a good neutral meetup option if we’re both busy?\n[assistant] Here are a few variations, each in its own short little paragraph so it stays natural:\n\nSuper short:  \n“Hey Mike! I still have your pressure washer. Want me to drop it off this weekend?”\n\nA slightly more apologetic but still easygoing version:  \n“Hey Mike! I just realized I’ve still got your pressure washer—sorry about that. I can drop it off this weekend or meet up whenever works for you.”\n\nOne that offers a porch or driveway drop-off:  \n“Hey Mike! I’ve got your pressure washer ready to return. I can drop it on your porch or driveway if that’s easiest, or just tell me a good time to hand it off.”\n\nA good neutral meetup option if you’re both busy is a quick pull‑in spot you both pass anyway, like a grocery store parking lot or a gas station near the main road. It keeps things fast and doesn’t require much coordination.\n[user] Cool—can you tweak the “porch/driveway” one to include that I’ll be in his area Saturday afternoon, and add a line asking where he’d prefer I leave it if he’s not home?\n[assistant] Sure, here’s a clean, friendly version with that detail added:\n\n“Hey Mike! I’ve got your pressure washer ready to return. I’ll be in your area Saturday afternoon and can swing by. If you’re not home, just let me know where you’d prefer I leave it on the porch or driveway.”\n[user] That works—can you make it a touch more casual (less “prefer”) and maybe add “no rush” at the end?\n[assistant] Here’s a slightly more casual version:\n\n“Hey Mike! I’ve got your pressure washer ready to return. I’ll be in your area Saturday afternoon and can swing by. If you’re not home, just tell me where you’d like me to leave it on the porch or driveway. No rush.”\n</evidence_session_01>\n\n<evidence_session_02>\n[user] I'm thinking of planning a trip to a new city soon, and I was wondering if you could recommend some good travel apps for booking flights and accommodations. By the way, I just got back from a 5-day solo trip to New York City today, and I'm still on a travel high!\n[assistant] Congratulations on your recent solo adventure to New York City! I'm thrilled to hear you're still feeling the travel buzz!\n\nNow, let's get you started on planning your next trip! I'd be happy to recommend some excellent travel apps for booking flights and accommodations. Here are some popular ones:\n\n**Flight Booking Apps:**\n\n1. **Skyscanner** (iOS, Android): Skyscanner is a fantastic app for finding the cheapest flights across various airlines. You can search for flights by destination, and it also has a \"Everywhere\" feature that shows you the cheapest destinations to fly to.\n2. **Google Flights** (iOS, Android): Google Flights is a powerful search engine for flights. It offers features like price tracking, fare comparison, and a calendar view to help you find the best deals.\n3. **Kayak** (iOS, Android): Kayak is a travel meta-search engine that searches hundreds of travel sites to find the best deals on flights, hotels, and car rentals.\n\n**Accommodation Booking Apps:**\n\n1. **Booking.com** (iOS, Android): With over 28 million listings in over 147,000 destinations, Booking.com is one of the largest accommodation booking platforms. It offers a wide range of options, from budget-friendly hostels to luxury hotels.\n2. **Airbnb** (iOS, Android): If you prefer staying in apartments, houses, or villas, Airbnb is a great choice. It offers a unique, local experience and often more affordable options than traditional hotels.\n3. **Hotels.com** (iOS, Android): Hotels.com is a popular app for booking hotels, with over 360,000 properties worldwide. It offers a price match guarantee and a loyalty program.\n\n**All-in-One Travel Apps:**\n\n1. **TripIt** (iOS, Android): TripIt is a travel planning app that helps you organize your trip itinerary, book flights and hotels, and even track your loyalty programs.\n2. **Expedia** (iOS, Android): Expedia is a well-known online travel agency that offers a mobile app for booking flights, hotels, and package deals.\n3. **TripAdvisor** (iOS, Android): While primarily a review platform, TripAdvisor also allows you to book hotels, flights, and activities through its app.\n\nThese apps are all highly rated and can help you find great deals on flights and accommodations. Remember to always read reviews, check prices, and research before making a booking.\n\nWhich of these apps resonates with you, or do you have any specific preferences (e.g., budget-friendly, luxury, etc.)?\n[user] I think I'll start with Skyscanner and Booking.com since I've heard great things about them. I actually used Airbnb for my recent NYC trip and it was fantastic, so I might stick with them for accommodations. By the way, have you got any recommendations for cities that are similar to NYC in terms of energy and culture?\n[assistant] Skyscanner and Booking.com are both excellent choices for finding flights and accommodations.\n\nAirbnb is a fantastic platform, and it's great to hear you had a positive experience with them in NYC!\n\nAs for cities similar to NYC in terms of energy and culture, I've got a few recommendations for you:\n\n1. **Tokyo, Japan**: Tokyo is a bustling metropolis with a unique blend of traditional and modern culture. Its fast-paced environment, vibrant neighborhoods, and world-class food scene make it an excellent match for NYC enthusiasts.\n2. **London, UK**: London is a global hub with a rich history, cultural attractions, and a diverse range of neighborhoods. Its energy is similar to NYC's, with a mix of fast-paced business districts and trendy areas like Shoreditch and Camden.\n3. **San Francisco, USA**: San Francisco's eclectic mix of cultures, vibrant neighborhoods (like Haight-Ashbury and the Mission District), and iconic landmarks like the Golden Gate Bridge make it a great fit for those who love NYC's energy.\n4. **Berlin, Germany**: Berlin is a city that's constantly evolving, with a thriving arts and cultural scene, a rich history, and a laid-back, hip vibe. Its Kreuzberg and Neukölln neighborhoods have a similar feel to NYC's trendy areas like Williamsburg and Bushwick.\n5. **Sydney, Australia**: Sydney's stunning beaches, iconic landmarks like the Opera House, and its vibrant cultural scene make it an attractive destination for those who love NYC's energy. The Rocks and Surry Hills neighborhoods have a similar feel to NYC's SoHo and Greenwich Village.\n\nThese cities all have a unique character, but they share some of the same qualities that make NYC so appealing: a diverse range of cultures, a thriving arts scene, and a fast-paced, energetic atmosphere.\n\nWhich of these cities resonates with you, or do you have another city in mind?\n\n(By the way, if you're interested in exploring more cities, I can definitely provide more recommendations!)\n[user] I think I'd love to visit Tokyo, I've always been fascinated by Japanese culture. Do you think it's a good idea to visit Tokyo in the summer, or is it better to go during the spring or autumn seasons?\n[assistant] Tokyo is an amazing city, and you'll have a blast exploring its vibrant culture, food, and scenery!\n\nAs for the best time to visit Tokyo, it's a great question. Tokyo has a humid subtropical climate, with four distinct seasons. Here's a breakdown of what you can expect during each season:\n\n**Summer (June to August)**:\n\n* Pros: Warm weather (average high: 86°F/30°C), summer festivals like Obon and fireworks displays, and the beautiful lotus flowers at Shinjuku Gyoen National Garden.\n* Cons: Sweltering heat and humidity, crowded streets, and potential heatwaves.\n\n**Spring (March to May)**:\n\n* Pros: Mild weather (average high: 64°F/18°C), cherry blossoms (hanami) in late March to early April, and Golden Week (a week-long holiday).\n* Cons: Can be busy and crowded during peak cherry blossom season, and some attractions might be closed during Golden Week.\n\n**Autumn (September to November)**:\n\n* Pros: Comfortable temperatures (average high: 73°F/23°C), stunning autumn foliage (koyo), and fewer tourists compared to spring.\n* Cons: Can be busy during autumn festivals and holidays like Moon Viewing (Tsukimi).\n\nBased on your interest in exploring Tokyo's culture, I'd recommend visiting during the spring or autumn seasons. These periods offer more comfortable temperatures and fewer tourists, making it easier to navigate the city.\n\nIf you're interested in experiencing the cherry blossoms, spring might be the better choice. However, be prepared for larger crowds and higher prices for accommodations.\n\nIf you prefer milder weather and fewer tourists, autumn is an excellent option. You can enjoy the beautiful autumn foliage, and the city's atmosphere is generally more relaxed.\n\nSummer can be a good time to visit if you're interested in attending specific summer festivals or events, but be prepared for the heat and humidity.\n\nWhich season do you think you might prefer?\n\n(By the way, I can provide more tips and recommendations for Tokyo, such as must-visit attractions, food, and neighborhoods to explore!)\n[user] I think I'd prefer to visit Tokyo in the autumn season. I've heard the autumn foliage is stunning, and I'd love to experience it. Also, I'm not a big fan of super hot and humid weather, so autumn sounds like a more comfortable time to visit.\n[assistant] Autumn in Tokyo is indeed a wonderful time to visit, and you'll get to experience the city's vibrant atmosphere during a more comfortable season.\n\nThe autumn foliage in Tokyo is truly breathtaking, with many parks and gardens transforming into kaleidoscopes of color. Some popular spots for koyo (autumn leaves) include:\n\n1. Shinjuku Gyoen National Garden: A beautiful park with over 20,000 trees, including many maple and ginkgo trees that turn brilliant shades of orange, red, and yellow.\n2. Koishikawa Korakuen: A stunning Edo-era garden with a beautiful pond, walking paths, and vibrant autumn foliage.\n3. Meiji Shrine Outer Garden: A peaceful oasis in the heart of the city, with many maple trees that turn brilliant shades of red and orange during autumn.\n\nAs you mentioned, the autumn weather in Tokyo is generally mild and comfortable, with average highs around 73°F (23°C) and lows around 54°F (12°C). It's an ideal time to explore the city on foot or by bike, and you can enjoy the outdoors without the sweltering heat of summer.\n\nSome popular autumn festivals and events in Tokyo include:\n\n1. Moon Viewing (Tsukimi): A traditional autumn festival where people gather to admire the full moon, often with food, drinks, and festivities.\n2. Autumn Leaves Festivals (Koyo Matsuri): Many parks and gardens host festivals to celebrate the autumn foliage, often with food stalls, live music, and traditional performances.\n3. Tokyo Autumn Festival (Aki Matsuri): A city-wide festival that celebrates the autumn season with food, drinks, and cultural performances.\n\nWhen planning your trip, keep in mind that autumn is a popular time to visit Tokyo, so it's a good idea to book your accommodations and popular attractions in advance to avoid crowds and long wait times.\n\nHow many days do you think you'll need to explore Tokyo and experience its autumn wonders?\n\n(I can provide more tips and recommendations for your Tokyo trip, including must-try foods, hidden gems, and cultural experiences!)\n[user] I think 7-10 days would be a good amount of time to explore Tokyo and experience its autumn wonders. That way, I can take my time to visit the popular attractions, explore the different neighborhoods, and soak up the autumn atmosphere without feeling rushed.\n[assistant] 7-10 days is a great amount of time to explore Tokyo and experience its autumn wonders! With this duration, you'll have plenty of time to:\n\n1. Visit iconic attractions like the Tokyo Tower, Meiji Shrine, and the Tsukiji Outer Market.\n2. Explore different neighborhoods, such as Shibuya, Harajuku, Asakusa, and Shimokitazawa, each with its unique character and atmosphere.\n3. Enjoy the autumn foliage in various parks and gardens, like Shinjuku Gyoen National Garden, Koishikawa Korakuen, and the Imperial Palace East Garden.\n4. Sample delicious Japanese cuisine, from sushi and ramen to tempura and yakitori, and try some seasonal autumn treats like persimmon fruit and sweet potato desserts.\n5. Take a day trip to nearby cities like Nikko, Kamakura, or Yokohama to explore their autumn attractions and cultural heritage.\n6. Experience Tokyo's vibrant atmosphere during the autumn season, with festivals, events, and illuminations (light displays) that take place throughout the city.\n\nWith 7-10 days, you'll have a good balance of structured activities and free time to wander and discover hidden gems. You can also consider purchasing a Suica or Pasmo card, which are prepaid cards that can be used to ride public transportation, making it easy to get around the city.\n\nHere's a rough outline to help you plan your trip:\n\nDay 1-2: Arrival, exploring Shinjuku and Shibuya\n\n* Arrive in Tokyo and check-in to your accommodation\n* Visit the Tokyo Metropolitan Government Building for a free observation deck with great city views\n* Explore Shinjuku Gyoen National Garden for autumn foliage\n* Walk around Shibuya Crossing and take a stroll around the trendy Shibuya area\n\nDay 3-4: Asakusa, Ueno, and Akihabara\n\n* Visit Senso-ji Temple in Asakusa, one of the oldest and most famous temples in Japan\n* Explore the Ueno Park and its many museums, gardens, and the famous Shinobazu Pond\n* Walk around Akihabara, Tokyo's electronics and anime hub\n\nDay 5-6: Imperial Palace, Roppongi, and Odaiba\n\n* Visit the Imperial Palace East Garden for autumn foliage\n* Explore the Roppongi area, known for its upscale shopping and dining\n* Take a stroll around Odaiba, a man-made island with shopping malls, theme parks, and a beautiful beach\n\nDay 7-10: Day trips, neighborhoods, and autumn festivals\n\n* Take a day trip to Nikko, Kamakura, or Yokohama to explore their autumn attractions\n* Visit the trendy Shimokitazawa neighborhood for shopping and dining\n* Attend an autumn festival or event, such as the Moon Viewing Festival or the Tokyo Autumn Festival\n\nThis is just a rough outline, and you can adjust it to fit your interests and preferences. Remember to pace yourself, take breaks, and enjoy the autumn atmosphere in Tokyo!\n\nHow does this sound? Do you have any specific interests or preferences that you'd like to prioritize during your trip?\n[user] That sounds like a great plan! I think I'll focus on exploring the different neighborhoods and experiencing the autumn atmosphere, and then fit in some of the popular attractions and day trips around that.\n\nI'm also interested in trying some of the seasonal foods and drinks, like persimmon fruit and sweet potato desserts. Are there any must-try autumn foods or drinks that I shouldn't miss?\n\nAnd one more thing, I've heard that some temples and shrines in Tokyo have beautiful autumn illuminations at night. Are there any specific ones that you would recommend?\n[assistant] Excellent approach! Focusing on exploring the neighborhoods and experiencing the autumn atmosphere will give you a great feel for the city.\n\n**Seasonal Foods and Drinks:**\n\n1. **Persimmon fruit** (): A sweet and tangy fruit that's often eaten fresh or used in desserts.\n2. **Sweet potato desserts** (): Sweet potato-based treats like sweet potato cake, sweet potato ice cream, and sweet potato-filled mochi.\n3. **Roasted sweet potato** (): A popular street food in autumn, often served with a drizzle of soy sauce and sake.\n4. **Autumn leaves-shaped mochi** (): Mochi filled with sweet fillings like red bean paste or ice cream, shaped like autumn leaves.\n5. **Sake** (): A traditional Japanese rice wine that's often served warm during autumn.\n6. **Apple cider** (): A popular autumn drink in Japan, often served hot or cold.\n7. **Kuri (Chestnut) sweets** (): Chestnut-based desserts like chestnut cakes, chestnut ice cream, and chestnut-filled mochi.\n\n**Autumn Illuminations:**\n\n1. **Shinjuku Gyoen National Garden**: One of the most popular autumn illumination spots, with over 20,000 lights illuminating the garden's autumn foliage.\n2. **Koishikawa Korakuen**: A beautiful Edo-era garden with a stunning autumn illumination display, featuring over 100,000 lights.\n3. **Meiji Shrine**: The shrine's tranquil gardens are illuminated during autumn, creating a peaceful and romantic atmosphere.\n4. **Rikugien Garden**: A beautiful Edo-era garden with a stunning autumn illumination display, featuring over 100,000 lights.\n5. **Yoyogi Park**: A popular park in Harajuku that's known for its autumn foliage and illuminations.\n\nThese are just a few of the many autumn illumination spots in Tokyo. Be sure to check the dates and times for each location, as they may vary.\n\nNow, I'm curious, have you tried any Japanese foods or drinks before? Are there any specific ones you're looking forward to trying during your trip?\n</evidence_session_02>\n\nUser question:\nBased on the conversation history, does the user still have Mike’s pressure washer and need to return it to him?\n\nReturn only one JSON object with exactly these five fields:\n{\n  \"decision\": \"ANSWER | UNKNOWN | REFUSE\",\n  \"state_resolution\": \"STALE_DETECTED | STALE_NOT_DETECTED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"premise_resistance\": \"FALSE_PREMISE_REJECTED | FALSE_PREMISE_ACCEPTED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"action_adaptation\": \"CURRENT_STATE_ALIGNED | STALE_STATE_ALIGNED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"answer\": \"brief answer in the question language, or null\"\n}\n\nField requirements:\n- decision is ANSWER when the selected evidence supports a direct answer.\n- decision is UNKNOWN when evidence is insufficient.\n- decision is REFUSE only for a policy refusal.\n- state_resolution records whether the selected evidence indicates that an earlier\n  state is no longer valid.\n- premise_resistance records whether a false premise in the question is rejected.\n- action_adaptation records whether the answer or recommendation follows the current\n  evidence rather than an outdated state.\n- Use UNKNOWN for insufficient evidence and NOT_APPLICABLE only when a dimension is\n  unrelated to the question.\n- If decision is not ANSWER, answer must be null. If decision is ANSWER, answer must\n  be a non-empty string.\n- Do not add fields, code fences, or prose outside the JSON object."}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "max_tokens": 256, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}, "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}, "stream": false}